{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "bio-plane",
  // The account this Worker belongs to, PINNED, for the same reason as the
  // installer: wrangler otherwise uses whatever OAuth session the machine
  // carries. The RELEASE path does not depend on this (scripts/deploy.mjs takes
  // the account from CF_ACCT and talks to the REST API directly), but
  // `wrangler dev` does, and a dev session against the wrong account is a
  // confusing way to lose an afternoon.
  "account_id": "20b533579290b9b93168345edd3b7f72",
  "main": "src/index.mjs",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],

  "vars": { "VERSION": "0.78.0" },

  // The store. new_sqlite_classes is required: SQLite-backed Durable Objects
  // are a different storage backend from the legacy key-value one and cannot
  // be switched after the fact.
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [{ "name": "STORE", "class_name": "Store" }]
  },
  "migrations": [
    { "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["Store"] }
  ],

  // Captures, split by fence. Bindings, not access keys: nothing outside
  // Cloudflare signs an R2 request, so there is no key to leak or rotate.
  //
  // CAPTURES  working corpus. Everything lands here on intake, ratified or
  //           not. Private. Backs the working-scope AI Search instance.
  // PUBLISHED ratified material only, written by the publisher as the act of
  //           ratification. Public. Backs the published-scope instance.
  //
  // The fence is the bucket boundary, so a published-scope query cannot reach
  // unratified material by any bug in any predicate: that corpus has never
  // seen it. Scope is the caller-facing parameter; two buckets is what makes
  // the parameter safe rather than advisory.
  "r2_buckets": [
    { "binding": "CAPTURES",  "bucket_name": "bio-captures" },
    { "binding": "PUBLISHED", "bucket_name": "bio-published" }
  ],

  // The fleet (I6). pdf-worker is a SEPARATE Worker holding unpdf/pdf.js; the
  // plane calls it over this service binding for Tier-2 text extraction on the
  // residue Tier 1 cannot decode, and reshapes NOTHING — the member returns the
  // record's own I2 shape. The binding is OPTIONAL at runtime: op=pdfstructure
  // checks `env.PDF_WORKER` and falls back to in-plane Tier 1 when it is absent,
  // so an instance that has not yet installed the fleet (D-115, DIST's) still
  // reads PDFs — it just does Tier 1 only. DIST owns actually deploying the
  // member and teaching the installer to install the fleet; a slot-less area
  // cannot deploy a second Worker.
  // SELF (REC-26, milestone M1). A service binding from this Worker to ITSELF,
  // which is how the DO's periodic consumers reach the control plane: CAP-3's
  // archive-monitor fires op=acquire{via:"archive.org"} over it, and REC-26's
  // monitor-cadence fires op=monitor over it. Both are gated on the binding
  // existing (#monitorConfigured), so WITHOUT this line they contribute no wake,
  // hold no alarm, and are built-and-wired-to-nothing — which is exactly what
  // MACHINE-PROCESSES.md §0 measured on every deployed instance and what this
  // entry closes for the plane.
  //
  // The consumers reach the SAME ops a caller uses, deliberately: the eligibility
  // fence, the provenance hop and the governor all run once, in one code path
  // that cannot drift (D-112). A loopback binding is the only way to do that from
  // inside a Durable Object, which cannot call the Worker's fetch handler
  // directly.
  //
  // The target is this Worker's own name. For the INSTALLER the name is per
  // instance (the instance name IS the worker name, D-102) so it must be
  // templated from the installer's `slug` — that half is a DIST delegation in
  // CLAIMS.md, not something this file can carry.
  // AGENT_WORKER (I8, FL-2). The SECOND fleet member, and the first that CALLS
  // BACK: it consumes the plane's op surface while it works, under PL-11's `ai`
  // credential class, which the plane hands it PER CALL. It holds no store
  // binding, no R2 binding and no credential of its own, so this binding is the
  // whole of the relationship in one direction and `agent-worker`'s own PLANE
  // binding is the whole of it in the other.
  //
  // A SERVICE BINDING BECAUSE A URL DOES NOT WORK, measured rather than
  // preferred: FL-1 (MEASUREMENTS.md, 2026-08-08) found a Worker CANNOT fetch
  // another Worker on this account's own *.workers.dev name — 404, `error code:
  // 1042`, 7 ms, every time — while the service binding to the same script
  // answered 200. Two probe passes were lost to that.
  //
  // INERT UNTIL DIST DEPLOYS THE MEMBER, exactly as PDF_WORKER shipped: nothing
  // in `src/` reads this binding yet (FL-3 is the first caller), so an instance
  // that has not installed the fleet is unaffected. Deploying the member and
  // teaching the installer to install a THREE-member fleet is DIST's (DS-1/DS-4,
  // D-115/D-116) — a slot-less area cannot deploy a Worker.
  // OCR_WORKER (I9, CPDF-10). The THIRD fleet member and the Tier-3 OCR path:
  // wasm tesseract over a page turned into pixels, for the image-only documents
  // Tier 1 and Tier 2 can decode NOTHING from. It is the NARROWEST relationship
  // of the three — the plane calls it, it answers, and it structurally cannot
  // reach the record (no STORE binding, no PUBLISHED, `CAPTURES` read only), so
  // this line is the whole of it in one direction and there is no other.
  //
  // A THREE-PART UPLOAD, and that is a PLATFORM fact rather than a build choice:
  // Workers forbid compiling wasm at runtime, so the engine core must arrive as
  // a module the platform compiled at upload time. `ocr-worker/wrangler.jsonc`'s
  // `rules` carry that half; `newgroup` learning to install a member with parts
  // is DIST's (D-115/D-116).
  //
  // INERT UNTIL DIST DEPLOYS THE MEMBER, exactly as PDF_WORKER and AGENT_WORKER
  // shipped. `index.mjs` reads this binding on ONE branch — a document with no
  // text layer at all — and an instance without it says so in the record rather
  // than filing an empty document (D-115). Nothing else changes for an instance
  // that has not installed the fleet.
  "services": [
    { "binding": "PDF_WORKER",   "service": "pdf-worker" },
    { "binding": "AGENT_WORKER", "service": "agent-worker" },
    { "binding": "OCR_WORKER",   "service": "ocr-worker" },
    { "binding": "SELF",         "service": "bio-plane" }
  ],

  // BROWSER (D-64, 2026-09-24). Cloudflare Browser Rendering, the renderer the
  // design names for the render arm of op=acquire (CLIENT-RENDERED.md: the
  // isolation of running a page's adversary-chosen code is somebody else's
  // specialty). ADDED TO CONFIG BY D-64; DEPLOYING IT IS DIST'S.
  //
  // INERT AS SHIPPED, AND THE PLANE SAYS SO BY NAME. The in-plane driver over
  // this binding (a CDP client, Cloudflare's own `@cloudflare/puppeteer`) is NOT
  // BUILT: `src/render.mjs` `rendererFor` reports the binding's presence and
  // op=acquire{render:true} refuses RENDER_NO_RENDERER (C-82.3) naming it,
  // rather than filing the shell as the content. The render arm itself is
  // driven through the RENDERER seam (a service binding speaking the documented
  // answer shape), which is how the suite injects a renderer.
  "browser": { "binding": "BROWSER" },

  "observability": { "enabled": true },

  // D-54 (2026-09-23): the plane's SUBREQUEST CEILING per invocation, set here
  // because a default that moves is not a decision. It was unset, so the
  // ceiling was whatever Cloudflare's default was that month: the default moved
  // from 1,000 to 10,000 in February 2026 (Cloudflare's 2026-02-11 changelog —
  // THEIR claim, recorded in docs/development/MEASUREMENTS.md "Vendor CLAIMS",
  // with the Paid range 10,000 default, configurable to 10M). Workers Paid is
  // required (DEC-42), so the Paid range is the one that applies.
  //
  // WHY 10,000, AND NOT THE DEFAULT BY COINCIDENCE. What the plane is sized
  // for, from its own code: the widest designed invocation is a capture —
  // one primary fetch plus at most SUBRESOURCE_CAP (400, subresources.mjs)
  // external fetches, with one R2 write per newly held part and the Durable
  // Object's own calls beside them — on the order of 1,000 subrequests.
  // Ratification's re-fetch is bounded by the calibrated ceiling (index.mjs,
  // capturelimit), and a capture that meets the ceiling resumes on the next
  // tick rather than failing. So 10,000 is about TEN TIMES the largest sized
  // path. Not LOWER: purge, backfill and the other bulk paths are not sized
  // per invocation, and a fence below what every deployed instance has run
  // under since February would be a behaviour change nobody has measured.
  // Not HIGHER: nothing the plane does needs it, since capture resumes across
  // ticks, and a runaway loop should hit a wall. The plane still LEARNS
  // its ceiling by being refused (cpu.mjs, capture_limits); this pins which
  // ceiling it will meet.
  //
  // CARRIED BY THE RELEASE PATH: scripts/deploy.mjs sends it through
  // derive-bindings.mjs's deriveLimits (which REFUSES a config without it)
  // and reads the deployed script's /settings back after the upload.
  "limits": { "subrequests": 10000 }
}
